_CALVIN_BIO = """
<p>John Calvin (1509&ndash;1564) was a French lawyer-turned-theologian who fled Catholic France for Protestant Geneva after his own conversion to the Reformation cause, and spent most of the rest of his life shaping that city into a working model of Reformed church and civic life. His central written legacy, the <em>Institutes of the Christian Religion</em> &mdash; first published in 1536 as a slim defense of persecuted French Protestants, then expanded across four editions into the definitive systematic treatment of Reformed doctrine by 1559 &mdash; remains one of the most influential single works in the history of Christian theology.</p>
<p>Calvin's theology is organized around the sovereignty and glory of God as the governing lens for everything else: salvation as God's initiative from beginning to end, the church's worship and government patterned directly on Scripture rather than tradition or convenience, and ordinary work and civic life treated as genuine arenas of service to God, not lesser concerns beside "spiritual" ones.</p>
<p><strong>Why he's part of Selah's roster:</strong> Calvin is Selah's single most cross-referenced voice &mdash; his arguments appear in fourteen separate topics, from the nature of grace and sin to worship, prayer, and covenant theology. Where a conversation touches classical Reformed thought, Calvin is very often the voice carrying that side of it.</p>
""".strip()

_LEWIS_BIO = """
<p>C.S. Lewis (1898&ndash;1963) taught medieval and Renaissance literature at Oxford and later Cambridge, and arrived at Christian faith reluctantly and gradually in his early thirties after years as a committed atheist &mdash; a conversion he described, memorably, as being dragged in "kicking and screaming" and finding himself, on the way, "the most reluctant convert in all England." That biography shows up directly in his writing: Lewis never argues as someone who has always found faith easy or obvious.</p>
<p>He's remembered for two very different bodies of work that both feed into the same project: popular apologetics (<em>Mere Christianity</em>, <em>The Problem of Pain</em>, <em>Miracles</em>) that explain classical Christian belief in plain, exact prose for general readers, and fiction (the Chronicles of Narnia, the Space Trilogy) that carries the same convictions in imaginative form.</p>
<p><strong>Why he's part of Selah's roster:</strong> Lewis is deployed across six different topics &mdash; sin, suffering, the problem of evil, spiritual warfare, Christology, and miracles &mdash; precisely because he writes for the same audience Selah is often talking to: an intelligent person taking Christian claims seriously for the first time, or returning to them after real doubt.</p>
""".strip()

_KUYPER_BIO = """
<p>Abraham Kuyper (1837&ndash;1920) may be the most unusual figure on Selah's roster simply because of how much he did at once: Dutch pastor and theologian, newspaper founder and editor, founder of a university, and eventually Prime Minister of the Netherlands (1901&ndash;1905) &mdash; all driven by one conviction he stated more memorably than almost anyone else in the tradition: "There is not a square inch in the whole domain of our human existence over which Christ, who is Sovereign over all, does not cry, 'Mine!'"</p>
<p>That conviction became a full theological framework he called <em>sphere sovereignty</em>: the idea that family, church, state, business, art, and science each have their own God-given authority, answerable directly to Christ rather than to each other &mdash; which is what let Kuyper argue, against both political withdrawal and theocratic control, that Christians are called to full, excellent engagement in every one of those spheres as an act of obedience.</p>
<p><strong>Why he's part of Selah's roster:</strong> Kuyper supplies the theological architecture that later voices like Tim Keller and Miroslav Volf build on when they talk about work and cultural engagement having real, permanent weight &mdash; Selah traces that thread back to where it actually starts.</p>
""".strip()

