_ARISTOTLE_BIO = """
<p>Aristotle (384&ndash;322 BC) studied for twenty years under Plato at Athens, later tutored the young Alexander the Great, and founded his own school, the Lyceum, where he built one of the most complete and influential bodies of thought in Western history &mdash; covering logic, biology, ethics, politics, metaphysics, and more. Unlike Plato, who looked past the physical world for ultimate reality, Aristotle insisted that careful observation of the actual world &mdash; how things grow, move, and change &mdash; was itself the starting point for understanding it.</p>
<p>His ethical framework, built around cultivating stable character (virtue) rather than simply following rules or calculating outcomes, and his method of drawing general principles from careful observation, shaped centuries of later thought &mdash; nowhere more consequentially than in medieval Christian theology, where thinkers like Thomas Aquinas built entire theological systems using Aristotelian categories.</p>
<p><strong>Why he's part of Selah's roster:</strong> Aristotle appears as premise-level background &mdash; the historical framework a theologian's argument is often built on top of, or occasionally arguing against &mdash; brought in only when a conversation pushes past a theological answer to the philosophical assumptions underneath it.</p>
""".strip()

_KIERKEGAARD_BIO = """
<p>S&oslash;ren Kierkegaard (1813&ndash;1855) spent nearly his entire life in Copenhagen, writing an enormous and strange body of work &mdash; much of it published under invented pseudonyms representing different points of view &mdash; that later thinkers would come to see as the beginning of existentialist philosophy. His central concern was the gap between believing true things <em>about</em> God and actually staking your life on a relationship <em>with</em> him &mdash; a distinction he thought the comfortable, state-sponsored Christianity of his own Denmark had almost entirely lost.</p>
<p>His best-known idea, the "leap of faith," isn't a claim that faith is irrational, but that genuine faith always involves a real, risk-bearing commitment that reason alone can bring you to the edge of but never make for you &mdash; a decisive act of the whole person, not just an intellectual conclusion.</p>
<p><strong>Why he's part of Selah's roster:</strong> Kierkegaard is deployed as premise-level background on what it actually means to hold a belief as one's own rather than as inherited convention &mdash; brought in when a conversation pushes past whether a doctrine is true into what it would mean to actually live as though it were.</p>
""".strip()

